                          uint32_t *actual_handles);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A channel has two ends, each a handle.
A message written to one end is read from the other, in order.
A message is up to `CROI_CHANNEL_MAX_BYTES` (65536) bytes and `CROI_CHANNEL_MAX_HANDLES` (64) handles; the handles move from the writer's table to the reader's.
A message with more of either is `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`.
Channels carry control and the transfer of handles; bulk data goes by shared memory and rings (vmo(2), port(2)).

An end asserts `CROI_SIGNAL_READABLE` when messages wait, `CROI_SIGNAL_WRITABLE` when it can be written and `CROI_SIGNAL_PEER_CLOSED` once the other end is gone; bind them to a port with object_bind(2).
object_get_info(2) gives the koid of the other end.

## channel_create

`channel_create` writes handles to the two ends to <out0> and <out1>.
<options> must be 0.
Each end has TRANSFER, WAIT, INSPECT, READ, WRITE and SIGNAL, but not DUPLICATE.
The job's policy for new channels applies.

## channel_write

`channel_write` writes a message for each of up to 64 items, each a `croi_channel_write_item_t` of 40 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t channel;
  uint32_t options;      // 0
  uint64_t bytes;        // const void *
  uint64_t handles;      // const uint32_t *: consumed
  uint32_t num_bytes;
  uint32_t num_handles;
  int32_t status;        // out: 0, or why this item failed
  uint32_t reserved;     // 0
} croi_channel_write_item_t;
```

The channel needs WRITE.
Each handle listed needs TRANSFER (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`) and may not be the channel written to (`CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`).
The handles are removed from the caller's table whether or not the item succeeds, unless the list itself cannot be read.
Items are all done: a failed one does not stop the later ones.
The call returns how many succeeded and writes each item's <status>.
An item whose channel's other end is closed has `CROI_ERR_PEER_CLOSED`.
A nonzero <options> or <reserved> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
Writing to an end whose peer holds more than 3500 unread messages still succeeds, and raises a policy exception (job(2)).

The first four bytes of a message are its transaction id, if it has four.
A message written to the peer of a channel that has a `channel_call` pending, whose first four bytes are that call's id, is the reply to the call and goes to the caller, not the queue.

`croi_channel_write` writes one message and returns the item's status or the call's.

## channel_read

`channel_read` reads a message for each of up to 64 items, each a `croi_channel_read_item_t` of 48 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t channel;
  uint32_t options;         // 0 or CROI_CHANNEL_READ_MAY_DISCARD
  uint64_t bytes;           // void *
  uint64_t handles;         // uint32_t *
  uint32_t num_bytes;       // the capacities
  uint32_t num_handles;
  uint32_t actual_bytes;    // out: the message's size, read or not
  uint32_t actual_handles;
  int32_t status;           // out: 0, or why this item failed
  uint32_t reserved;        // 0
} croi_channel_read_item_t;
```

The channel needs READ.
The next message is copied to <bytes> and its handles are added to the caller's table, their values written to <handles>, each with the rights it had.
<actual_bytes> and <actual_handles> are set to the message's size on success and on `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`.
A message larger than the capacities stays queued, unless <options> is `CROI_CHANNEL_READ_MAY_DISCARD`, which drops it.
Any other option is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.
With no message the item fails with `CROI_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`, or `CROI_ERR_PEER_CLOSED` if the other end is closed; messages written before the close are read first.
Items are all done.
The call returns how many succeeded.

`croi_channel_read` reads one and returns the item's status or the call's.

## channel_call

`channel_call` writes a request and waits for its reply.
<channel> needs READ and WRITE and <options> must be 0.
The kernel sets the first four bytes of the request, which must be at least four bytes long (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`), to a transaction id of its own, with the top bit set, and returns the reply whose first four bytes are the same.
<args> describes the request and the room for the reply:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t wr_bytes;    // const void *
  uint64_t wr_handles;  // const uint32_t *
  uint64_t rd_bytes;    // void *
  uint64_t rd_handles;  // uint32_t *
  uint32_t wr_num_bytes;
  uint32_t wr_num_handles;
  uint32_t rd_num_bytes;
  uint32_t rd_num_handles;
} croi_channel_call_args_t;
```

The request's handles are consumed as for `channel_write`, except that an unreadable <args> consumes none.
The reply's sizes are written to <actual_bytes> and <actual_handles>; a reply that does not fit the capacities is dropped and the call fails with `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL`.
The call waits until <deadline> (`CROI_ERR_TIMED_OUT`), or until the other end closes (`CROI_ERR_PEER_CLOSED`), or until the caller is killed (`CROI_ERR_CANCELED`).
While the caller waits, the thread that read the request runs with the caller's scheduling parameters, until it replies.
The thread the request wakes, through a port, is placed on the caller's CPU, and the caller on the replier's when the reply comes, if nothing else is waiting to run there and no CPU with more capacity is idle; the two then take turns on one CPU.

